WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:17.920
Good afternoon, everybody.

00:17.920 --> 00:40.040
I got something to show you.

00:40.040 --> 00:43.480
Think about this as a pixel, a flying pixel.

00:43.480 --> 00:46.600
This is what we call in our lab sensible design.

00:46.600 --> 00:48.240
Let me tell you a bit about it.

00:48.240 --> 00:52.460
If you take this picture, I'm Italian originally, and every boy in Italy grows up with this

00:52.460 --> 00:55.160
picture on the wall of his bedroom.

00:55.160 --> 00:59.680
But the reason I'm showing you this is that something very interesting happened in Formula

00:59.680 --> 01:02.700
One racing over the past couple of decades.

01:02.700 --> 01:07.880
Now some time ago, if you wanted to win a Formula One race, you take a budget and you

01:07.880 --> 01:12.960
bet your budget on a good driver and a good car, and if the car and the driver were good

01:12.960 --> 01:15.440
enough, then you win the race.

01:15.440 --> 01:20.040
Today, if you want to win the race, actually you need also something like this.

01:20.040 --> 01:25.800
Something that monitors the car in real time, has a few thousand sensors collecting information

01:25.800 --> 01:31.360
from the car, transmitting this information into the system, and then processing it and

01:31.360 --> 01:36.720
using it in order to go back to the car with decisions and changing things in real time

01:36.720 --> 01:38.860
as information is collected.

01:38.860 --> 01:43.680
This is what in engineering terms you would call a real-time control system.

01:43.680 --> 01:49.800
And basically, it's a system made of two components, sensing and an actuating component.

01:49.800 --> 01:54.200
What is interesting today is that real-time control systems are entering, starting to

01:54.200 --> 01:56.120
enter into our life.

01:56.120 --> 02:02.820
Our cities, over the past few years, just have been blanketed with networks, electronics.

02:02.820 --> 02:07.080
They're becoming like computers in open air, and as computers in open air, they're starting

02:07.080 --> 02:12.220
to respond in a different way, to be able to be sensed and to be actuated.

02:12.220 --> 02:14.300
If we fix cities, actually it's a big deal.

02:14.300 --> 02:19.660
Just as an aside, I wanted to mention cities are only 2% of the Earth's crust, but they

02:19.660 --> 02:22.500
are 50% of the world's population.

02:22.500 --> 02:28.780
They're 75% of energy consumption, up to 80% of CO2 emissions.

02:28.780 --> 02:32.220
So if you're able to do something with cities, that's a big deal.

02:32.220 --> 02:39.220
Beyond cities, all of this, sensing and actuating, is entering our everyday objects.

02:39.260 --> 02:44.620
That's from an exhibition that Paolo Antonelli is organizing at MoMA later this year, during

02:44.620 --> 02:48.780
the summer, it's called Talk to Me, where our objects, our environment, is starting

02:48.780 --> 02:50.380
to talk back to us.

02:50.380 --> 02:55.520
In a certain sense, it's almost as if every atom out there were becoming both a sensor

02:55.520 --> 03:00.540
and an actuator, and that is radically changing the interaction we have as humans with the

03:00.540 --> 03:02.660
environment out there.

03:02.660 --> 03:07.600
In a certain sense, it's almost as if the old dream of Michelangelo, when Michelangelo

03:07.600 --> 03:11.960
sculpted the Moses, at the end it's said that he took the hammer, threw it at the Moses,

03:11.960 --> 03:18.960
actually you can still see a small chip underneath, and said, shouted, why don't you talk?

03:19.280 --> 03:23.400
Well today, for the first time, our environment is starting to talk back to us.

03:23.400 --> 03:28.040
And I'll show just a few examples, again with this idea of sensing our environment

03:28.040 --> 03:29.720
and actuating it.

03:29.720 --> 03:31.000
Let's start with sensing.

03:31.000 --> 03:35.540
Well, the first project I wanted to share with you is actually one of the first projects

03:35.540 --> 03:36.540
by our lab.

03:36.540 --> 03:41.980
Which was four and a half years ago in Italy, and what we did there was actually use a new

03:41.980 --> 03:46.580
type of network at the time that had been deployed all across the world, that's a cell

03:46.580 --> 03:50.740
phone network, and use anonymous aggregated information from that network that's collected

03:50.740 --> 03:55.900
anyway by the operator in order to understand how the city works.

03:55.900 --> 04:02.160
The summer was a lucky summer, 2006, it's when Italy won the soccer World Cup.

04:02.160 --> 04:05.820
Some of you might remember it was Italy and France playing, and then Zidane at the end

04:05.820 --> 04:09.780
the head-butting, anyway, Italy won at the end.

04:09.780 --> 04:16.780
Now, look at what happened that day just by monitoring activity happening on the network.

04:17.300 --> 04:24.300
Here you see the city, you see the Colosseum in the middle, the river Tevere, it's morning

04:25.560 --> 04:31.900
before the match, you see the timeline on the top, early afternoon, people here and

04:31.900 --> 04:35.080
there making calls and moving.

04:35.080 --> 04:41.820
The match begins, silence, France scores, Italy scores, half-time, people make a quick

04:41.820 --> 04:48.820
call and go to the bathroom, second half, end of normal time, first overtime, second,

04:49.120 --> 04:56.120
Zidane the head-butting in the moment, Italy wins, yeah.

04:58.480 --> 05:03.300
Well that night everybody went to celebrate in the center, you saw the big peak, the following

05:03.320 --> 05:08.820
day again, everybody went to the center to meet the winning team and the Prime Minister

05:08.820 --> 05:12.860
at the time, and then everybody moved down, you see it in the image at the place called

05:12.860 --> 05:17.440
Chirco Massimo, where since Roman times people go to celebrate, to have a big party and you

05:17.440 --> 05:19.380
see the peak at the end of the day.

05:19.380 --> 05:23.600
Well that's just one example how we can sense the city today in a way that we couldn't have

05:23.600 --> 05:25.800
done just a few years ago.

05:25.800 --> 05:30.600
Another quick example about sensing, it's about, not about people, but it's about things

05:30.600 --> 05:32.120
we use and consume.

05:32.120 --> 05:37.460
Well today we know everything about where our objects come from, this is a map that

05:37.460 --> 05:43.420
shows you all the chips that form a Mac computer, how they came together, but we know very little

05:43.420 --> 05:44.960
about where things go.

05:44.960 --> 05:51.100
So in this project we actually developed some small tags to track trash as it moves through

05:51.100 --> 05:52.140
the system.

05:52.140 --> 05:57.900
So we actually started with a number of volunteers who helped us in Seattle just over a year

05:57.900 --> 06:04.800
ago to tag what they were throwing away, different type of things as you can see here, things

06:04.800 --> 06:11.400
they would throw away anyway, then we put a little chip, little tag onto the trash and

06:11.400 --> 06:13.320
then started following it.

06:13.320 --> 06:19.060
Here are the results we just obtained.

06:19.060 --> 06:27.480
From Seattle.

06:27.480 --> 06:53.720
Another one week.

06:53.720 --> 06:57.560
With this information we realised there's a lot of inefficiencies in the system, we

06:57.560 --> 07:02.120
can actually do the same thing with much less energy, this data was not available before

07:02.120 --> 07:06.100
but there's a lot of wasted transportation and convoluted things happening.

07:06.100 --> 07:10.260
But the other thing is that we believe that actually if we see everyday that the cap we

07:10.260 --> 07:14.460
are throwing away, it doesn't disappear, it's still somewhere on the planet and the plastic

07:14.460 --> 07:18.560
bottle we are throwing away everyday still stays there and we show that to people, then

07:18.560 --> 07:20.960
we can also promote some behavioural change.

07:20.960 --> 07:26.000
So that was the reason for the project, my colleague could tell you much more about sensing

07:26.000 --> 07:29.720
and many other wonderful things we can do with sensing but I wanted to go to the second

07:29.720 --> 07:33.760
part we discussed at the beginning and that's actuating our environment.

07:33.760 --> 07:39.240
And the first project is something we did a couple of years ago in Zaragoza, Spain.

07:39.240 --> 07:44.400
It started with a question by the mayor of the city who came to us saying that Spain

07:44.400 --> 07:50.040
and southern Europe have a beautiful tradition of using water in public space, in architecture,

07:50.040 --> 07:54.400
and the question was how could technology, new technology be added to that?

07:54.400 --> 07:57.600
And one of the ideas was developed at MIT in a workshop was imagine you've got this

07:57.600 --> 08:03.880
pipe and you've got valves, solenoid valves, tabs, opening and closing you create like

08:03.880 --> 08:09.940
a water curtain with pixels made of water, those pixels fall and you can write on it,

08:09.940 --> 08:15.120
you can show patterns, images, text and even you can approach it and it will open up to

08:15.120 --> 08:19.400
let you jump through as you see in this image.

08:19.400 --> 08:23.800
Well we presented this to Mayor Belioc, he liked it very much and we got a commission

08:23.800 --> 08:26.560
to design a building at the entrance of the expo.

08:26.560 --> 08:34.920
We called it Digital Water Pavilion, the whole building is made of water.

08:34.920 --> 08:38.800
There's no doors or windows but when you approach it, it will open up to let you in.

08:49.400 --> 09:00.260
The roof also is covered with water and if there's a bit of wind, if you want to minimize

09:00.260 --> 09:07.400
splashing you can actually lower the roof or you could close the building and the whole

09:07.400 --> 09:11.080
architecture will disappear like in this case.

09:11.080 --> 09:14.160
You know these days they always get emails during the winter when they take the roof

09:14.160 --> 09:17.800
down of people who've been there and say you know they demolished the building and no they

09:17.800 --> 09:22.480
didn't demolish it, just when it goes down the architecture almost disappear.

09:22.480 --> 09:28.000
Here's the building working, you see the person puzzled about what was going inside.

09:28.000 --> 09:32.880
Here it was myself trying not to get wet, testing the sensors that opened the water.

09:32.880 --> 09:38.120
Well I should tell you now what happened one night when all of the sensors stopped working

09:38.120 --> 09:41.080
but actually that night it was even more fun.

09:41.080 --> 09:44.760
All the kids from Zaragoza came to the building because the way of engaging with the building

09:44.760 --> 09:46.000
became something different.

09:46.000 --> 09:49.680
Not anymore a building that would open up to let you in but a building that would still

09:49.680 --> 10:07.080
make cuts and holes through the water and you had to jump without getting wet.

10:07.080 --> 10:12.080
And that was for us was very interesting because as architects, as engineers, as designers

10:12.080 --> 10:16.400
we always think about how people will use the things we design but then reality is always

10:16.400 --> 10:21.640
unpredictable and that's the beauty of doing things that are used and interact with people.

10:21.640 --> 10:27.000
Here is an image then of the building with the physical pixels, the pixels made of water

10:27.000 --> 10:31.240
and then projections on them and this is what led us to think about the following project

10:31.240 --> 10:32.640
I'll show you now.

10:32.640 --> 10:35.600
That's imagine those pixels could actually start flying.

10:35.600 --> 10:40.760
Imagine you know you could have small helicopters that move in the air and then each of them

10:40.760 --> 10:46.360
with a small pixel and changing lights almost as a cloud that can move in space.

10:46.360 --> 10:53.960
Here is the idea.

10:53.960 --> 11:06.600
So imagine one helicopter like the one we saw before moving with others in synchrony

11:06.600 --> 11:15.680
so you can have this cloud.

11:15.680 --> 11:21.160
You can have a kind of flexible screen or display like this, a regular configuration

11:21.160 --> 11:33.840
in two dimensions or in regular but in three dimensions where the thing that change is

11:33.840 --> 11:47.040
the light, not the pixels position.

11:47.040 --> 11:51.480
You can play with different type, imagine your screen could just appear in different

11:51.480 --> 12:09.200
scale or sizes, different type of resolution but then the whole thing can be a 3D kind

12:09.200 --> 12:16.200
of cloud of pixels that you can approach and move through it and see from many directions.

12:16.200 --> 12:21.600
Here is the real fly fire control and going down from the regular grid as before.

12:21.600 --> 12:25.420
When you turn on the light actually you see this, so the same we saw before and imagine

12:25.420 --> 12:27.400
each of them then controlled by people.

12:27.400 --> 12:31.680
You can have each pixel having an input that comes from people, from people's movement

12:31.680 --> 12:32.680
or so on.

12:32.680 --> 12:36.280
So I wanted to show you something here for the first time is we've been working with

12:36.280 --> 12:41.760
Roberto Bolle, one of today's top ballet dancers, the Etoualles at Metropolitan in New York

12:41.760 --> 12:47.120
and Lascale in Milan and actually captured his movement in 3D in order to use it as an

12:47.120 --> 12:48.760
input for fly fire.

12:48.760 --> 12:54.560
And here you can see Roberto dancing.

12:54.560 --> 12:59.320
You see on the left the pixels, the different resolution being captured, it's both 3D scanning

12:59.320 --> 13:03.680
in real time and motion capture.

13:03.680 --> 13:08.280
So you can reconstruct the whole movement.

13:08.280 --> 13:17.040
You can go all the way to...

13:17.040 --> 13:20.740
But then once you have the pixels then you can play with them and play with color and

13:20.740 --> 13:25.080
movement and gravity and rotation.

13:25.080 --> 13:47.720
So we want to use this as one of the possible inputs for fly fire.

13:47.720 --> 13:50.080
I wanted to show you the last project we are working on.

13:50.080 --> 13:52.680
It's something we're working on for the lantern Olympics.

13:52.680 --> 13:53.680
It's called the cloud.

13:53.680 --> 13:58.720
And the idea here is imagine that, again, we can involve people in doing something and

13:58.720 --> 14:04.320
changing our environment and almost doing what we call cloud raising, like barn raising

14:04.320 --> 14:05.320
but with a cloud.

14:05.320 --> 14:08.520
Imagine you can have everybody make a small donation for one pixel.

14:08.520 --> 14:13.340
And I think what is remarkable that happens over the past couple of years is that over

14:13.340 --> 14:18.160
the past couple of decades we went from the physical world to the digital one.

14:18.160 --> 14:22.520
This has been digitizing everything, knowledge and making that accessible through the internet.

14:22.520 --> 14:26.980
Now today for the first time, and the Obama campaign showed us that, we can go from the

14:26.980 --> 14:32.120
digital world, from the self-organizing power of networks to the physical one.

14:32.120 --> 14:36.560
This can be in our case we want to use it for designing and doing a symbol, doing something

14:36.560 --> 14:38.280
built in a city.

14:38.280 --> 14:43.240
But tomorrow it can be in order to tackle today's pressing challenges, think about climate

14:43.240 --> 14:47.800
change or CO2 emission, how we can go from the digital world to the physical one.

14:47.800 --> 14:52.280
So the idea that can we actually involve people in doing this thing together collectively,

14:52.280 --> 14:57.280
our cloud is a cloud again made of pixel in the same way as a real cloud is a cloud

14:57.280 --> 15:00.600
made of particles and those particles are water.

15:00.600 --> 15:04.700
Where our cloud is a cloud of pixel, is a physical structure in London but covered with

15:04.700 --> 15:05.700
pixel.

15:05.700 --> 15:09.200
You can move inside with different type of experiences, you can actually see it from

15:09.200 --> 15:16.560
underneath, sharing the main moments for the Olympics in 2012 and beyond and really using

15:16.560 --> 15:18.740
it as a way to connect with the community.

15:18.740 --> 15:26.940
So both physical cloud into the sky and something you can go to the top like a London's new

15:26.940 --> 15:33.120
mountain top, you can enter inside it and a kind of new digital beacon for the night

15:33.120 --> 15:38.260
but most importantly a new type of experience for anybody who will go to the top.

15:38.260 --> 15:38.540
Thank you.

